From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emilwojcik93 <34631176+emilwojcik93@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 14:52:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] display: adapt resolution, frame rate and bitrate to the
 display

New option "Adapt to the display" (default on). The stream mode follows the
display the stream is shown on, at every stream start and, through the existing
restart path, when that display changes (connection, resolution, refresh rate,
primary display). The saved resolution, frame rate and bitrate are the reference.

- Resolution: native size of the display, always landscape (a rotated 2-in-1
  keeps the stream shape), even numbers, capped at 3840x2160.
- Frame rate: largest standard rate not above the refresh rate, capped at 144
  (a rate far above the nearest standard one, such as 48 Hz, is kept).
- Bitrate: a value the user never changed follows Moonlight's default for the
  new mode; a user-set value is a ceiling, scaled 0.4x to 1.0x.
- Stream display: primary display (default), the Moonlight window's display
  (the previous behaviour), or a display by output name.
- A running stream samples the display once a second and restarts after the
  wanted mode has differed for 3 s, at most once every 20 s; not for GFE hosts.
- Settings: live status of the target display and the stream now (resolution,
  frame rate, codec, bitrate, HDR), Auto (value) on the resolution and frame rate
  rows, display list. The command line keeps its own values unless
  --auto-adapt-display is given.

Policy and debounce gate are pure code (backend/displayautoconfig) with unit
tests; the session and the settings page share backend/displayadapt.
